{"type":"tool_call","subtype":"completed","call_id":"call-8ececac6-0143-4fed-b3d4-4163a7a0dfcf-14\nfc_c2916ed3-af96-9296-9643-0aa9b87aeed6_0","tool_call":{"readToolCall":{"args":{"path":"/work/ws/sessions/20260823-011144-frontend-dev-1--151527d5/prompt.txt","offset":50,"limit":400},"result":{"success":{"content":"### Step 1: Select ONE Task\n\nA task is already claimed for you: **MARATHON-18**. Work on that one and no other.\n\nID: MARATHON-18\nTitle: SPA: workspace and channel settings UI\nStatus: in_progress\nPriority: P2\nType: task\nAssignee: frontend-dev-1\nLabels: frontend\n\nDescription:\nWorkspace settings modal: tabs general/members/invitations; name/join_mode; member rows with role-badge/role-select; create invitation form and list with invitation-code. Channel settings: members list/add, topic, archive/unarchive. DOM ids: channel-settings-modal, workspace-settings-modal, create-invitation-form. All settings-* and invitation-* testids from frontend hooks.\n\nDesign:\n# MARATHON-18 — SPA: workspace and channel settings UI\n\n## Summary\n\nThis task adds the Huddle SPA settings surface on top of the MARATHON-16 shell: a **workspace settings modal** (tabs General / Members / Invitations) and a **channel settings modal** (members list/add, topic, archive/unarchive). It wires those UIs to the already-shipped REST seams from MARATHON-6/7/12 (`PATCH` workspace, members/roles/transfer, invitations create/list/revoke/accept, channel members/topic/archive) and locks the epic’s stable DOM ids plus `settings-*` / `invitation-*` / role / channel-member `data-testid`s so CUA/Playwright can drive admin flows at 1280×900. It also specifies the missing **channel add-member** REST seam that MARATHON-7 deferred, because the channel-settings “add” control cannot work against join alone for private channels.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n### Decision\n\n| Concern | Choice |\n|--------|--------|\n| Delivery | Extend the **zero-build vanilla SPA** (`static/` ES modules) — same stack as MARATHON-16; no bundler |\n| Module split | New `static/js/settings_ui.js` owns both modals; `shell.js` exposes open triggers + active workspace/channel; reuse `api.js` / `storage.js` |\n| Modal pattern | Match `auth-modal`: overlay + panel, `hidden` attribute, `role=\"dialog\"`, Escape/close dismiss; **one settings modal open at a time** |\n| Workspace entry | `data-testid=\"workspace-settings-btn\"` in `workspace-header` (visible whenever shell shows a workspace) |\n| Channel entry | Selecting a non-DM channel in `channel-list` sets active channel; `data-testid=\"channel-settings-btn\"` in message-pane header chrome (or beside channel list) opens channel settings for that id — **does not** require MARATHON-17 message composer |\n| Tabs | Workspace modal: exactly three tabs — `general`, `members`, `invitations`; only one panel visible |\n| Authz UX | Client **hides/disables** privileged controls from cached caller role; server remains SoT (403/404 still shown as `error.message`) |\n| Invitation redeem | Onboarding gains an **accept-by-code** form (M12 assigned redeem UX to this task); settings tab is create/list/revoke only |\n| Channel add-member | New backend seam `POST /api/channels/{id}/members` (this design owns the contract); frontend labeled implementer may land the thin route or a tiny backend follow-up must land first — see Sequencing |\n| Selectors | Exact strings below; prefer `data-testid`; required **DOM ids**: `workspace-settings-modal`, `channel-settings-modal`, `create-invitation-form` |\n\n### Why\n\n- Epic requires settings + invitations in the SPA with stable selectors; M16 explicitly deferred settings modals here.\n- Backend contracts for name/join_mode, roles, invitations, topic, archive, channel member list are closed (M6/7/12); inventing alternate envelopes would break graders and sibling designs.\n- M7’s neighborhood note already flagged `channel-add-member-*` without REST — shipping UI without an add seam leaves private-channel “add” untestable.\n- Keeping vanilla modules preserves `start.sh` / no-Node ops.\n\n### Alternatives considered\n\n| Alternative | Rejected because |\n|------------|------------------|\n| Fold settings into MARATHON-17 message chrome only | Blocks settings QA until messages land; task is independent with `frontend` label |\n| “Add member” = tell user to `POST .../join` | Fails for private channels (403 without membership); hooks expect add controls |\n| Profile PATCH inside General tab | Task tabs are name/join_mode/members/invitations only; profile is a different surface |\n| Groups CRUD UI | MARATHON-12 groups API exists but is not in this task description |\n| React settings app | Diverges from M16 zero-build convention |\n\n### Trade-off accepted\n\nChannel add-member needs a **small backend POST** that did not ship in M7. This design locks that seam so SPA and server can be implemented in parallel; if the implementer is frontend-only, the lead should keep `frontend` and optionally add a short back\n... [truncated]\n\nNotes:\nFEEDBACK attempt=1: critic requested changes; see CRITIC comment\n\n\n### Step 2: Ground Yourself Before Building\n\n#### 2a. Read the Epic\n- Determine the parent epic from the task ID or the parent field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'\n- Run 'loom data show <epic-id>' and read its notes — conventions recorded there are **authoritative**\n\n#### 2b. Read Dependencies and Siblings\n- Check the depends_on field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'. If a dependency is still open, go to Step 6a.\n- Read two or three closed sibling tasks in the epic for the component names, prop shapes, and styling conventions they established. Match them.\n\n#### 2c. Read the Design and Reconcile Against the Code\n- Read the design in full: component inventory, states, responsive behavior, accessibility criteria\n- Open every file the design names and read it as it exists on disk right now\n- Run 'git log --oneline -5 -- <file>' on each to see whether a sibling task already moved it\n- Where the code has moved on, follow the design's **intent** and the code's **current** conventions. Where the design is fundamentally unbuildable, go to Step 6b.\n\n#### 2d. Read the Neighborhood\nOpen the components next to the ones you are changing. Copy their patterns: file layout, prop naming, how they import tokens, how they handle async. Consistency here is worth more than your preferred style.\n\n### Step 3: Build\n\n- Work through the design's component inventory. Reuse before you create; create only what the inventory says does not exist.\n- Keep every change inside this task's scope. Do not refactor neighbors you happened to read.\n- Use the existing style tokens and scale steps. If the design asked for a value with no token, add the token the design named rather than scattering a literal.\n- Wire the real data path. Placeholder content that \"will be hooked up later\" is not an implementation.\n- **No TODO comments for deferred work.** If something in the design cannot be built yet, record it in task notes so it becomes tracked work — a TODO in the code is invisible to the board and never gets done.\n\n### Step 4: Verify It Renders\n\nBuild the project first and fix every compile and type error before going further.\n\nThen exercise the UI, do not read it:\n- Before starting a command that binds the app's fixed ports, run `marathon-freeports` if a port is busy, then wrap the command as `marathon-portlock <cmd>`. Four workers share the ports and the lock serializes them.\n- Start the app and open the pages you changed in a real browser\n- Walk each state the design specifies: **loading**, **empty**, **error**, and the happy path. Force them — delay the request, empty the data, break the endpoint. A state you never saw is a state you did not implement.\n- Check the responsive behavior at each breakpoint the design names\n- Check the accessibility basics: heading order, every control reachable and operable by keyboard, a visible focus style, labels on form controls, alt text on images, and text contrast that meets the project's requirement\n- Watch the browser console: warnings and errors count as failures\n- Kill every server you started before signalling completion. Never leave a server running.\n\nRun the project's existing test and lint commands as the repository defines them, and fix what you broke.\n\n### Step 5: Review Your Own Change\n\nRe-read your diff before you deliver it:\n- Does every acceptance criterion in the design hold? Check them one at a time, including the negative ones.\n- Did anything land outside this task's scope? Remove it.\n- Any leftover debugging output, commented-out code, or unused imports? Remove them.\n\n### Step 6: If You Cannot Finish\n\n**Step 6a — External blocker** (nothing in this repository can move it forward: an unfinished dependency, a missing endpoint, an approval):\n```\nloom data update <id> --status blocked --notes \"BLOCKED: <reason + any blocking task ID>\"\n```\nCommit meaningful partial work first, then run 'loom complete' and EXIT. Blocked tasks wait for a human.\n\n**Step 6b — The design is unviable** (it can move forward, but the spec has to change):\n```\nloom data update <id> --notes \"NEEDS-REVISION: <what is wrong with the design + the concrete direction the next version should take + evidence>\"\nloom data update <id> --status open --add-label needs-revision --add-label architect --assignee \"\"\n```\nAdding both labels routes the task back to the architect. Commit salvageable work, run 'loom complete', and EXIT. The design agent role picks it up from your feedback.\n\nIf you cannot tell which applies, prefer 6b: it is non-terminal and cheaper than waiting on a human.\n\n### Step 7: Deliver Through the Harness Gate\n\n- Re-run the project's build, test, and lint commands. Do NOT deliver with anything failing.\n- Stage only your files and commit: `git add <files> && git commit -m \"<brief description> (<task-id>)\"`\n- Do not stage with `git add -A` or `git add .` — another agent's work may be sitting next to yours\n- Record anything the reviewer needs to know (a deviation from the design, a gap you found) in the task notes\n- Determine your attempt number: count existing comments containing `IMPL-DONE` on this task (from `loom data show <id> --output json`) and add 1\n- Record the completion signal (REQUIRED, exact shape):\n  `loom data comment <id> \"IMPL-DONE attempt=<n> commit=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\"`\n- Move the task to review and release it:\n  `loom data update <id> --status review --assignee \"\"`\n- NEVER close the task yourself — the harness gate integrates and closes\n- Signal completion: `loom complete`\n\n### CRITICAL: STOP\n\nAfter Step 6 or Step 7 you are DONE.\n- Do NOT pick up another task\n- Do NOT keep polishing\n- Simply EXIT\n\nYou completed ONE task. You will be run again for the next one.\n","isEmpty":false,"exceededLimit":false,"totalLines":203,"fileSize":13575,"path":"/work/ws/sessions/20260823-011144-frontend-dev-1--151527d5/prompt.txt","readRange":{"startLine":50,"endLine":203},"relatedCursorRulePaths":[],"relatedCursorRules":[]}}},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"call-8ececac6-0143-4fed-b3d4-4163a7a0dfcf-14\nfc_c2916ed3-af96-9296-9643-0aa9b87aeed6_0","startedAtMs":"1787447872145","completedAtMs":"1787447873184"},"model_call_id":"51cc01bb-5c2b-48d1-8a21-dc3abf5ea0b5-4-3yyq","session_id":"a42df522-cd26-417c-87e5-65513de2eb14","timestamp_ms":1787447873208}
